herefore, the range is the set $\\{1,2\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507490 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ed/edc4005c3c0a8f3d521156afc9d38c3ac35ee140c4479928d4c1ffb08fda21ad.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "beacaecf-2eb5-55f8-8278-e9b484096e26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507490 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs at the point $(-2,1).$\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $5.$ This occurs at the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $1$ and $5,$ including both $1$ and $5.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $1\\le y\\le 5,$ which can also be written as  $1\\le f(x)\\le 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
